```java
int i = 5;
int a = ++i;  // pre-increment: i becomes 6, a = 6
System.out.println(i + " " + a);  // 6 6

int j = 5;
int b = j++;  // post-increment: b = 5, then j becomes 6
System.out.println(j + " " + b);  // 6 5

// Classic gotcha:
int x = 3;
int y = ++x + x++;  // ++x makes x=4, y starts as 4; x++ uses 4, then x=5; y = 4+4 = 8
System.out.println(x + " " + y);  // x=5, y=8
```

```java
// && short-circuits: if left is false, right never runs
String s = null;
if (s != null && s.length() > 0) {  // Safe! s.length() not called if s is null
    System.out.println("non-empty");
}

// || short-circuits: if left is true, right never runs
if (isAdmin() || hasPermission()) {  // hasPermission() not called if isAdmin() is true
    grantAccess();
}

// & and | are non-short-circuit — ALWAYS evaluate both sides
if (s != null & s.length() > 0) {  // DANGER: s.length() called even if s is null!
    // throws NullPointerException if s is null
}
```

```java
// if-else statement
String label;
if (age >= 18) {
    label = "adult";
} else {
    label = "minor";
}

// Ternary expression — same result, fewer lines
String label = age >= 18 ? "adult" : "minor";

// Use inline in print or method call
System.out.println(score >= 60 ? "Pass" : "Fail");

// Nested ternary — technically valid but hard to read
String grade = score >= 90 ? "A" : score >= 80 ? "B" : "C";
```

The ternary operator is best for simple, single-value decisions. Avoid nesting more than one level.

```java
// String concatenation gotcha
System.out.println("Sum: " + 1 + 2);    // "Sum: 12" — NOT "Sum: 3"
System.out.println("Sum: " + (1 + 2));   // "Sum: 3" — parentheses force arithmetic first
System.out.println(1 + 2 + " is sum");   // "3 is sum" — arithmetic first, then string concat

// Common precedence order (high to low):
// 1. () parentheses
// 2. ++, -- (unary)
// 3. *, /, %
// 4. +, -
// 5. <, >, <=, >=
// 6. ==, !=
// 7. &&
// 8. ||
// 9. = (assignment)
```
